\subsection{Per-axis regularizer optimum and deployed anisotropy}
\label{sec:adaptation-rs-anisotropy}

The reduced-MSE optimum is scalar but applies independently to each axis.  Let
$q_i$ be the low-frequency posterior acceleration-error intensity and
$m_{-4,i}$ the negative fourth moment of the \emph{true} displacement spectrum
for axis $i\in\{x,y,z\}$.  From
Eq.~\eqref{eq:adapt-reduced-mse-pole},
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,i}^{\star}
 \propto q_i^{1/14}m_{-4,i}^{3/7}T_S^{-1/2},
\end{equation}
and therefore the anisotropic ratios relative to the vertical channel are
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\rho_x^{\star}&:=\frac{r_{S,x}^{\star}}{r_{S,z}^{\star}}
=\left(\frac{q_x}{q_z}\right)^{1/14}
 \left(\frac{m_{-4,x}}{m_{-4,z}}\right)^{3/7},
\\[2pt]
\rho_y^{\star}&:=\frac{r_{S,y}^{\star}}{r_{S,z}^{\star}}
=\left(\frac{q_y}{q_z}\right)^{1/14}
 \left(\frac{m_{-4,y}}{m_{-4,z}}\right)^{3/7}.
\end{aligned}}
\label{eq:adapt-rs-axis-ratios}
\end{equation}
This expression explains why anisotropy cannot be chosen from drift intensity
alone.  Horizontal tilt/gravity leakage can make $q_x$ and $q_y$ much larger
than $q_z$, but the exponent is only $1/14$.  The desired-motion term carries
exponent $3/7$ and therefore has six times the logarithmic weight.  The
amplitudes and spectra of surge and sway enter through $m_{-4,x}$ and
$m_{-4,y}$: a channel containing more genuine low-frequency displacement is
penalized more strongly for a tight $S=0$ anchor.

That dependence also makes fixed world-axis factors problematic for marine
motion.  Surge and sway are projections of the directional orbital field into
the vessel/world frame, so their amplitudes change with heading and encounter
direction.  A pair of constants $(\rho_x,\rho_y)$ inferred from one heading
would generally not remain the same after rotating the vessel relative to the
same sea.  A common horizontal multiplier cannot remove this issue; it merely
forces two different desired-motion spectra to share one compromise.

The scalar ratios are diagnostics, not a complete-estimator tuning law. The
current vessel-response study retains separate horizontal regularization
factors, while the stationary OU-acceleration prior remains isotropic. The
implemented default is
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\vct r_S
 &=r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
   \begin{bmatrix}\rho_x&\rho_y&\rho_z\end{bmatrix}^{\top},\\
\rho_x&=0.72,\qquad \rho_y=0.50,\qquad \rho_z=1,\\
\mat R_S
 &=r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}^2
   \diag\!\left(\rho_x^2,\rho_y^2,1\right).
\end{aligned}}
\label{eq:fus-rs-anisotropy}
\end{equation}
The factors multiply standard deviations, not covariance entries. The scalar
$r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}$ is the applied vertical value after the common clamp
and cadence conversion in
Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation-target-implementation}. The independent OU-prior
setting is
\begin{equation}
 (\matg{\Sigma}_{aw}^{\mathrm{stat}})^{1/2}
 =\sigma_{aw,\mathrm{appl}}\mat I_3 .
\end{equation}
Thus an isotropic acceleration prior does not imply an isotropic integral
pseudo-measurement covariance.

These factors are applied in world NED coordinates and are not rotated with
heading. The reference vessel-response records hold yaw at zero, so the two
horizontal axes coincide with surge and sway only in that validation setting.
Section~\ref{sec:results} discusses the observed axis-dependent response and
the complete-estimator comparisons. The selected factors are empirical for
that ensemble; they are neither the exact scalar optima
$\rho_x^{\star},\rho_y^{\star}$ nor a heading-invariant hull model.
Heading-dependent rotation of the regularization covariance and validation
under changing encounter directions remain separate extensions.
